4. Significant work

A Larger Work is a significant work in its own right if it provides
substantial functionality, beyond the Package, that its users would
value even if it did not Expose an Interface of the Package.

A Larger Work is merely a facade over the Package if its primary
purpose or effect is to make the Package's functionality available
under another name, packaging, or user interface. Examples are a
program that launches the Package and forwards its ports, or a
program whose main added feature is a replacement user interface for
the Package's own modem functions.

For example, these are significant works in their own right:

  - a messaging, email, or file-transfer client that uses the Package
    as its modem and also accepts connections from other programs on
    the Package's ports;
  - a station-control, logging, or digital-modes suite that includes
    the Package among its modes;
  - a mobile application that runs the Package for its own features
    and also lets other applications on the device use its ports.

These are not:

  - a rebranded or repackaged build of the Package whose ports are its
    main reason to exist;
  - a "Data2G server" or "Data2G app" that adds little beyond starting
    the Package, configuring it, and relaying its interfaces.

